mechaber.en:
We already said that a witness who said "I heard that so-and-so died" — even if he heard from a woman who heard from a slave — he is fit for a woman's testimony and they permit on his word.

{Rama: And even if he did not say from whom he heard but said simply "I heard" — he is fit; and we are not concerned lest the first was disqualified.}

But if the witness or the woman or the slave said "so-and-so died" and "I saw him die" — they question him: how did you see and how did you know? If he testified with a clear matter he is believed; but if he testified with matters that mostly indicate death — they do not permit his wife; for one does not testify that a person died unless they saw him die with certainty and there is no doubt.

{Rama: If they heard a voice of lamentation that mentioned him among the dead and eulogized him — they permit his wife (so too the Maggid Mishneh in the name of the Tosefta). Therefore one should protest to women not to eulogize a person based on estimations that prove he died, and they should not eulogize him until they know he died; and likewise his wife is forbidden to eulogize him or wear black as long as there is no testimony fit to permit her (Seder HaDorot and so too the Rivash siman 508). And likewise a court should not give a testimony document to a woman for what witnesses testified before them if that testimony is not fit to permit her — unless they wrote explicitly the reason they did not permit her on this testimony, lest damage come from a court that is not expert (there in the responsum of the Rivash).}

We already said that a Samaritan who speaks offhandedly — they permit based on him. How was he speaking offhandedly? He was conversing offhandedly and said "woe to so-and-so who died, how handsome he was and how much good he did with me"; or he was conversing and saying "when I was coming on the road and so-and-so who was walking with us fell and died, and we wondered at this thing how he died suddenly" — and the like of these matters that show his intention was not to testify — he is believed. And an Israelite who heard from a Samaritan speaking offhandedly testifies that he heard from him and his wife may marry on his word.

{Rama: And likewise if he said "so-and-so died" speaking offhandedly — even though he did not say other words it is called properly mesalfei lefi tumo (Rivash siman 377) (and some are stringent) (Beit Yosef in the name of the Ran).}

In what case? When there was no ulterior motive; but if there was an ulterior motive in the Samaritan's conversation — lest he intended otherwise, such as if he said to one "do such-and-such for me lest I kill you as I killed so-and-so" — this is not speaking offhandedly, for his intention is to instill fear for idolatry.

{Rama: And likewise in a place where there is concern for falsehood that they taught him to say thus — one does not rely on him (R' Yonah Netiv 23).}

And likewise if he heard from courts of Samaritans that said "we killed so-and-so" — they are not believed; for they hold themselves up with falsehood. And likewise all similar matters.

{Rama: And some say all courts are not believed even if they say he was killed in judgment not by their hand (Tur in the name of the Rosh); and all this is specifically regarding courts; but other Samaritans speak offhandedly and say he was judged in courts of Samaritans — they are believed (Piskei Maharai siman 34).}

A Samaritan and an Israelite went from us to another place and the Samaritan came and said "a man who went from here with me died" — they permit his wife even though the Samaritan does not know that man. And some say he must say "I buried him."

{Rama: And specifically in this manner where he does not mention the name of the deceased and does not recognize him; but if he recognizes him we do not require "I buried him" in mesalfei lefi tumo (Magid Mishneh according to Rambam and so too Maharai in his rulings siman 223 and Beit Yosef in the name of the responsum of the Ran and Rivash). And likewise the custom spread — even in a place where some say we require "I buried him" — it does not mean literally "I buried him" but anything he says whose implication is that he certainly died and he did not say in a manner of doubt suffices (Beit Yosef in the name of the responsum of the Ran and Rivash).}

And likewise if ten men went together from place to place and they were bound in a collar or carrying camels and the like, and the Samaritan spoke offhandedly and said that ten men who went from such-and-such place to such-and-such place and were carrying such-and-such all died — they permit their wives. And some say he must say "we buried them."

And likewise a woman to whom one testified that her husband drowned in endless waters and did not come up and his memory was lost and his name forgotten — behold she may not marry on this testimony as explained (and even if the court permitted her and she did not marry — she may not marry) (Rivash siman 379). And if she married she need not leave.

{Rama: And specifically if she married based on a sage or in error thinking she was permitted; but if she married wantonly she must leave (responsum of Maharam brought by Mordechai end of Yevamot). And all this is specifically regarding one about whom they testified he actually drowned in endless waters; but one about whom they testified he was on a ship that broke at sea or the like, or even that he drowned but they did not testify he remained in the water long enough for his soul to depart — she must leave. And even if he testified simply that he drowned — we are concerned lest he called such things "drowning" as is the way of the world to call these things drowning — and she must leave unless he testified explicitly that he actually drowned and remained long enough for his soul to depart (Beit Yosef in the name of the responsum of Ramban siman 128).}

And even if it was a Samaritan who spoke offhandedly and said so-and-so drowned and she married on his word — behold she need not leave. And a sage who ruled to permit her ab initio (in endless waters) — they excommunicate him.

One witness came and testified that her husband died and they permitted her to marry on his word; and afterward one witness came and contradicted the first and said he did not die — behold she need not leave the permission and may marry; for one witness is believed for a woman's testimony like two witnesses for other testimonies, and the statement of one is not in place of two. However, because of slander she should not marry (Tur).

But if the second came before they permitted her — behold she may not marry. And if she married she must leave because it is safek. And if she married before the witness testified for her and she says "it is clear to me he died" — behold she need not leave. And some say she must leave. And if two came and said he did not die — even though she married she must leave according to all.

{Rama: And specifically when she is silent; but if she also says "died" it is like two against two if the witnesses are women; but the husband who married her is not believed regarding himself (Nimukei Yosef chapter HaIshah Kama).}

In what case? When the one she married based on is like two who came and contradicted him — such as she married based on one man and two came and said he did not die; or she married based on a woman or on herself and two women or two disqualified persons of their words came and said he did not die. But a fit witness says "died" and many women say "did not die," or disqualified persons say "did not die" — this is like half and half.

{Rama: And specifically when they came together before they permitted her based on the first fit witness; but if they permitted her based on the fit witness and afterward the disqualified ones came — she need not leave the first permission (Tur).}

And if she married one of the witnesses and she says "it is clear to me he died" — behold she need not leave.

{Rama: And Samaritans speaking offhandedly — their law is like other disqualified testimony (Mordechai chapter HaIshah Batra). And whoever does not speak offhandedly is not believed neither to forbid nor to permit.}
